  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through next Sunday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: An upper level low currently centered\nover the Pacific NW is helping to draw up moisture from southern\nCA/NV. Rain showers began in southern White Pine County around\n1AM and are expected to expand north and west the rest of today \nand tonight. QPF amounts remain on track with much of central and\nnortheastern NV projected to receive between a half inch and an\ninch of rain by Monday morning. Latest guidance has backed off on\nthe wind speeds across central NV between late this morning and\nearly this evening. Left the wind advisory in effect however as\nwinds are expected to pick back up later this evening into early\nMonday morning. The moist southerly flow across central and\nnortheastern Nevada will keep snow levels at or above 10,000 feet\nthrough this afternoon. This evening as the low over the Pacific\nNW begins shifting south and east toward northern NV snow levels \nacross NV will begin to lower. For most of central Nevada as well \nas eastern Elko County snow levels are expected to remain at or \nabove 8500 feet through 5 AM Monday. The cooler air from the \napproach Pac NW low will however bring snow levels across north- \ncentral Elko County down to around 6000-6500 feet by sunrise \nMonday. Lingering showers in combination with the cooler air mass\nmay result in light snow Monday morning at Wild Horse, Mountain \nCity, Owyhee and Jarbidge. Most of this area will likely see less \nthan a quarter inch of snow Monday morning, however Jarbidge could\nsee an inch or more before temperatures climb later Monday \nmorning. Monday night the low coming in from the Pac NW is \nexpected to be centered directly over central Nevada. Snow levels\nMonday night will lower to around 6000 feet. Summits and higher \nelevation valley locations across most of the CWA are projected to\nreceive between a trace and a half inch of snow into Tuesday \nmorning. Slightly higher amounts of an inch or so are possible \nalong summits of Highway 50 including the town of Eureka. Showers \nlinger Tuesday afternoon with snow levels rising to around 7500 \nfeet. Showers end Tuesday evening with a dry period expected \nWednesday through next weekend. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: Decreased confidence in wind\nspeeds across central NV today and tonight. High confidence \nregarding significant rainfall in central and eastern Nevada \nthrough Monday. High confidence in a cooling trend through Monday \nnight. Low to moderate confidence in snow levels and amounts \nMonday into Tuesday.",
